          %h2#discussion Discussion
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Here is a degenerate loop:
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi e
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                              %mi e
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↓
                          %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                            %mi e
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp; \stackrel{e}{\to} &amp; X \\
                  {} &amp; \mathllap{\scriptsize{e}}{\nwarrow} &amp; \darr\scriptsize{e} \\
                  {} &amp; {} &amp; X
                  }
          %p
            To say this loop commutes, is to say
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^3 = 1_X
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: That is correct.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Since this loop is degenerate, we may or may not have (depending on convention?), parallel identity arrows running between each vertex.
          %p
            If there are hidden identity morphisms, then to say the triangle commutes is to say
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = 1_X
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: No: the above version
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^3 = id
              is correct.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Here is a degenerate triangle:
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi e
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                              %mi e
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↓
                          %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                            %mi e
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp; \stackrel{e}{\to} &amp; X \\
                  {} &amp; \mathllap{\scriptsize{e}}{\searrow} &amp; \darr\scriptsize{e} \\
                  {} &amp; {} &amp; X
                  }
          %p
            To say this triangle commutes is to say
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: That is correct.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Since this triangle is degenerate, we may or may not have (depending on convention?), parallel identity arrows running between each vertex.
          %p
            If there are hidden identity morphisms, then to say the triangle commutes is to say
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = 1_X
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
            \: No: the above version
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
            is correct. For both of these wrong statements, you are assuming that arrows somehow cancel. Especially the first one, think of it in terms of dimensional analysis. If you have a quantity in physics with dimensions of something else cubed, it cannot have the dimensions of that original thing (unless both are dimensionless). But this is only a rough analogy, please don’t read anything deep into it. In my comment in reply to Harry’s
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
            being a loop, then I said I would draw this as a commuting triangle. I wouldn’t draw a loop (i.e. an endomorphism) (resp. a degenerate loop) as a triangle (resp. commuting triangle) in any circumstances, because we are not given that the loop factors into a composite of other arrows.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Thanks David. I think I’ve managed to boil it down to the basic disagreement. It is about “shape dependence”. I reject the notion of shape dependence. The morphism
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to X
            is a loop and this loop is the same as
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\righttoleftarrow
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=332&Focus=7368#Comment_7368"} explain this
            on the n-Forum. The point is, you can accept shape dependence, in which case I agree with everything you say. Or you reject shape dependence and work with semidiagrams instead.
          .query
            %p
              I don't think that there's any doubt that
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^2 = e
              is the intended interpretation of the second diagram by everybody who uses commutative diagrams. The fact that the same object
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              shows up twice is irrelevant; the whole point of putting
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in there twice is because you
              %em don't
              want parallel identity arrows. Of course, you can invent an alternative interpretation if you want, but I don't think that you'll find any usage to match it in the wild. —Toby
          %h2#main_article Main Article
          %p A stub for now.
          %p The basic goal of this page is to turn Domenico’s and Urs’ statements about connections into statements about commutative diagrams.
          %p The rough is idea is:
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext Higher Connection Flat
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext Higher Diagram Commutes
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Higher Connection Flat}\quad\quad\simeq\quad\quad\text{Higher Diagram Commutes}
          .standout
            %p
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=723&Focus=5015#Comment_5015"}> Domenico
              \: I guess this is well known, but let me try writing it here and see what happens.
            %p
              If a connection on a principal G-bundle is locally represented by the 1-form
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              with values in
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F=d\omega+\frac{1}{2}[\omega,\omega]
              vanishes, that is, if
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is a solution of the Maurer-Cartan equation.
            %p
              Now, what is interesting is that one can see curvature only on 2-dimensional paths (it is a 2-form): if one restricts
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              to a 1-dimensional submanifold, then there
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is clearly a solution to the Maurer-Cartan equation. so, if I think of an infinitesimal 1-simplex and look at my connection there, I could say that my connection is 1-flat. then, moving to an infinitesimal 2-simplex I see that the connection is (generally) not 2-flat: holonomy along two sides of the 2-simplex is not the same thing as holonomy along the third side. not the same, but in a very precise way: the curvature
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FF
              exactly measures the gap to go from a horn of the 2-simplex to the third edge. this is very 2-categorical, and suggests I could cure the lack of flatness of my original connection by adding a copy of
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              in degree -1 and cooking up a 2-Lie algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔩
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}_0
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔩
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}_{-1}
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo +
                    %mi δ
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\omega+\frac{1}{2}[\omega,\omega]+\delta F=0
              where
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔩
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔩
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta\colon \mathfrak{l}_{-1}\to \mathfrak{l}_{0}
              is the differential of the
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo −
                    %mi algebra
                    %mi 𝔩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty-algebra \mathfrak{l}
              (and it should be induced by the identity of
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}[-1]
              to
              = succeed ")." do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              had curvature
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is now equivalent to say that
              %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega+F
              is flat.
            %p in other words, what seemed a non-flat connection was so since I was not seeing the 2-bundle, but only a 1-bundle approximation. and on a 1-bundle I can only clearly see up to 1-simplices, wher my connection was actually flat.
            %p
              once curvature has come in, we can repeat the argument: now we have a 2-flat connection and test it on the 3-simplex. if it has 3-curvature, that will presumibly be because we are not seeing the 3-bundle, yet. so I find it natural to wonder (to conjecture) whether any connection on a principal bundle (and more generally any
              = succeed "-flat" do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-bundle." do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .standout
            %p
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=723&Focus=5050#Comment_5050"}> Domenico
              \: I think I could take the task of working a bit on oo-Lie algebroid valued forms in the direction sketched above. yet, I’d like to still discuss it a bit here, sicne I’m now thinking of some more radical change. what I’m thinking is that the one-step definition as it is now is too strict. I mean, we are now defining flat
              = succeed "-connections" do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_470db1e2bd243a7b103b684bcde179db6c95d92f_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
